<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adamawa Plateau</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Adamaoua Plateau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Adamawa Plateau is an upland region of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> extending into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a>, rising to over 1,000 metres between the southern forests and the northern savannas. It takes its name from Modibo Adama, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> leader who conquered much of the area in the early 19th century as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>. Its grasslands support <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> herding, and Ngaoundéré is its main town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adamawa-plateau.jpg" alt="Adamawa Plateau" width="320" height="181" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hills_near_Ngaoundal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
